<!-- ================= PERMISSION ================= -->
<section class="band band--wash" id="permission">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="col">
      <p class="eyebrow rise">The question you already searched</p>
      <h2 class="wide rise">Is this allowed — and is my prayer worth less?</h2>
      <p class="rise">Most people look this up before they look for anything to buy. We are not scholars and we won't pretend to be. Here is what the texts say, with the references, so you can check them yourself and take them to someone qualified.</p>

      <h3 class="rise">On praying seated when you can't do otherwise</h3>
      <p class="rise">The Prophet ﷺ told 'Imran ibn Husayn: pray standing; if you cannot, then sitting; and if you cannot, then on your side — <em>Sahih al-Bukhari 1066</em>. Ibn Qudamah records in <em>al-Mughni</em> (1/443) that the scholars are unanimous that a person unable to stand may pray sitting. And Allah does not burden a soul beyond its capacity (2:286), and asks that we keep our duty to Him as much as we are able (64:16).</p>

      <h3 class="rise">On the half the reward narration</h3>
      <p class="rise">There is an authentic narration that one who prays sitting receives half the reward of one who stands — <em>Sahih al-Bukhari 1115–1116</em>. If you know this hadith, it has probably been sitting at the back of your mind every time you considered making things easier on yourself.</p>
      <p class="rise">Read what the scholars did with it.</p>

      <div class="note rise">
        <p><strong>Al-Nawawi</strong>, in <em>al-Majmu'</em> (4/226), states that the ummah is agreed that whoever is genuinely unable to stand in an obligatory prayer may pray sitting, does not repeat it, and <strong>his reward is not reduced — because he is excused.</strong> He grounds this in a second authentic narration in al-Bukhari: when a person is sick or travelling, there is written for him the reward of what he used to do when he was healthy and at home.</p>
        <p><strong>Al-Khattabi</strong>, quoted approvingly by Ibn Hajar in <em>Fath al-Bari</em> (2/468), reads the halving the same way: it addresses the sick person who is <em>still able</em> to stand with difficulty. The reward is halved to encourage him to stand — while still permitting him to sit.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="src rise" style="margin-top:14px">So there are two narrations, not one. The first is about the person who could stand and chose not to. The second is about you.</p>

      <h3 class="rise">Two things worth knowing about this stool in particular</h3>
      <p class="rise"><strong>Your sujud stays on the ground.</strong> Nothing is placed under your forehead — which matters, because there is a narration in which the Prophet ﷺ removed a pillow a sick man had put down to prostrate on. Some prayer chairs are sold with a shelf or tabletop for exactly that purpose. This has nothing of the kind, by design.</p>
      <p class="rise"><strong>And you stay low.</strong> Every authority we read on accommodation — across more than one madhhab — preferred that the worshipper remain close to the ground rather than be raised up onto furniture. 25cm is a great deal closer to the ground than a chair is.</p>

      <div class="note rise">
        <h3 style="margin-top:0">Now do the thing we would do</h3>
        <p>Take this page, or the references on it, to your own imam and ask him about your own case. He knows your madhhab and he knows you. We don't, and it would be wrong of us to answer as though we did.</p>
        <p><a href="/blogs/journal/is-it-allowed-to-pray-sitting-down" style="color:var(--gold-a)">The longer write-up, with all the references in one place →</a></p>
        <p style="margin-bottom:0"><em>We'll never pretend to be scholars. We just make the seat.</em></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= THE MAT ================= -->
<section class="band band--white">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="col">
      <p class="eyebrow rise">If you already tried something</p>
      <h2 class="wide rise">You bought the thick mat. It didn't fix this.</h2>
      <p class="rise">Almost everyone reading this has — memory foam, orthopaedic, the expensive one your daughter found. It helped a little. And you may have quietly concluded that nothing will.</p>
      <p class="rise">Here's why it didn't work, and it isn't your fault.</p>
      <p class="rise">A mat changes what the floor feels like against your knee. That's a real benefit for the sajdah itself. But it cannot change the <em>position</em> your legs are in during the jalsa. Sitting back on your heels closes the knee to its tightest angle and puts your body weight on top of it. Something soft underneath doesn't open that angle. Nothing you lay on the floor can.</p>
      <p class="rise">There's a second reason, and one of the mat makers says it on their own website: they deliberately keep the foam thin where your forehead lands — well under an inch — so the sujud stays properly in contact with the ground. They can't make it thicker there without creating a religious problem.</p>
      <p class="rise"><strong>The mat category has gone as far as it can go, and the ceiling is the floor.</strong> You didn't fail at using it. It was solving a different problem.</p>
      <p class="rise"><a href="/blogs/journal/do-thick-prayer-mats-help-knee-pain">More on why thick prayer mats only fix half of it →</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

