<section class="band">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="col">
      <p class="eyebrow rise">If you're buying this for a parent</p>
      <h2 class="wide rise">He'll tell you he's fine.</h2>
      <p class="rise">He has said it every time. You've watched the hand go out to the wall, watched the pause before he gets up, watched him sit down a little too carefully at Eid — and been told, every time, that it's nothing.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="pair rise">
      <figure style="margin:0">
        <img src="{{ 'sajda-img-08.jpg' | asset_url }}" alt="A woman standing in a doorway watching her father pray in the next room.">
      </figure>
      <div>
        <h3>Send the answer before you send the stool</h3>
        <p>A parent almost never refuses this kind of thing out of pride about the object. They refuse because of the quiet fear that sitting makes their prayer worth less — and most people carry that fear for years without ever saying it out loud. <a href="#permission">The section above</a> is written to be read by them, or forwarded, or printed off. Send that first. Let the answer arrive before the parcel does.</p>

        <h3>Then the words matter more than the gift</h3>
        <p>Not <em>"I got you this because you're struggling."</em> Try:</p>
        <p class="script">"This is for the sitting between the sajdahs. You still stand, you still put your head on the floor — it just takes your weight off your ankles. Have a look and tell me if it's any good."</p>
        <p>That does three things at once: it makes the difficulty specific and mechanical rather than general and declining, it makes clear that nothing about the prayer is being given up, and it asks their opinion instead of announcing a decision.</p>
        <p style="margin-bottom:0">Serving your parents is its own act of worship. It's also, in this case, a 25cm object that will sit under a prayer mat and never once announce itself.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

